.title = "Argument Parsing with structargs",
.date = "2024-01-01",
.author = "ZigCC",
.layout = "section.shtml",
---

Parsing arguments is common in command line programs and there are some packages in Zig to make this
task easier. To name a few:

- [Hejsil/zig-clap](https://github.com/Hejsil/zig-clap)
- [MasterQ32/zig-args](https://github.com/MasterQ32/zig-args/)
- [structargs](https://zigcli.liujiacai.net/)

[Manual parsing](13-01-argparse) is a good fit for small programs with a few options. For larger or
more complex command line interfaces, a mature argument-parsing library is usually a better choice
because it can provide consistent help text, validation, subcommands, and error handling.

Here we use `structargs` from the [zigcli](https://github.com/jiacai2050/zigcli) toolkit. This
package maps command line options to fields in a Zig struct.

